INT. BOTANICAL GARDEN, ORCHID HOUSE - NIGHT

Mist hangs in humid air. Violet and white blooms spill from hanging baskets. A path of wet gravel winds between benches.

FENN, 40s, in a damp raincoat, crouches beside a bench and runs a gloved thumb along a stem. CALLIOPE, 30s, the greenhouse curator, stands over him, arms folded, with a keyring that jingles at her hip.

He lifts a petal with tweezers. She watches his hands.

                    FENN
          This one's been cut.

                    CALLIOPE
          Orchids are cut all the time.

                    FENN
          Not at the root. Not with a scalpel.

She says nothing. He bags the stem and places it carefully in a case.

                    CALLIOPE
          You won't find anything here.

                    FENN
          You're sure?

                    CALLIOPE
          I'm certain.

He stands. The mist parts around him. He looks up at the glass ceiling, then back at her, and his eyes stop on her gloved hand.

                    FENN
          Who taught you to hold a scalpel like that?

                    CALLIOPE
          ^Who taught you to ask?

They speak in perfect unison. A drop of water falls from a leaf onto the gravel. Neither moves.
